{
  "groups": [
    {
      "heading": "[508] config: hosts was REJECTED, now ACCEPTED",
      "answers": {
        "verdict_changes": true,
        "direction": "looser",
        "stored_value_changes": false,
        "already_signed_off": false,
        "real_clients": "yes",
        "why": "Inputs that zod 3 rejected on hosts[i] are now accepted, so this is a one-directional loosening (probe H1/H2). Root cause: src/schemas.ts replaces the ip check with z.union([z.ipv4(), ipv6, zonedIpv6]), and zonedIpv6 only requires a '%' with a non-empty suffix and a valid ipv6 prefix. zod 3's z.string().ip() was much narrower: its ipv6 regex only allowed a zone index on fe80: link-local addresses with an alphanumeric zone (probe Z3: fe80=true, 2001:db8::1%eth0=false, fe80::1%eth0%eth1=false). So the merged build admits whole classes zod 3 never matched: zoned global/unspecified addresses ('2001:db8::ff00:42:8329%wlan0', '::%1') and malformed zones ('fe80::1%eth0%eth1', probe H3). review/corpus-diffs.md section [4] signs off restoring zoned IPv6 for the ip field only, on the explicit factual premise that 'the union reproduces exactly the set zod 3 matched ... so nothing *new* is let in' - the probes show that premise is wrong, and hosts is never mentioned in the review at all (the corpus is 400 signup requests). Therefore not signed off. Judgement: real_clients = yes, because hosts comes from environment config and an operator or OS tool can legitimately write a scoped address; but the same judgement does not excuse the garbage-zone cases, and a human should decide whether to narrow zonedIpv6 to the fe80 + alphanumeric-zone shape zod 3 actually accepted."
      },
      "claims": [
        {
          "claim": "config.hosts entries that zod 3 rejected (zoned IPv6) are accepted by the merged build.",
          "probe": "const r=o=>o.ok?'ACCEPT':'REJECT';\nconst cfg=h=>({hosts:h,timeoutMs:1000,port:'8080'});\nprint('H1 '+r(oracle.parseConfig(cfg(['2459::52d2%lo0'])))+' '+r(main.parseConfig(cfg(['2459::52d2%lo0']))));\nprint('H2 '+r(oracle.parseConfig(cfg(['96.87.107.24','a644::8654%eth0'])))+' '+r(main.parseConfig(cfg(['96.87.107.24','a644::8654%eth0']))));",
          "expect": "H1 REJECT ACCEPT"
        },
        {
          "claim": "zod 3's z.string().ip() accepted a zone index only on fe80 link-local addresses with a clean zone, so the merged union lets in addresses zod 3 never matched.",
          "probe": "const Z3=zod3.z||zod3; const ip=Z3.string().ip();\nprint('Z3 fe80='+ip.safeParse('fe80::1%eth0').success+' other='+ip.safeParse('2001:db8::1%eth0').success+' badzone='+ip.safeParse('fe80::1%eth0%eth1').success);",
          "expect": "Z3 fe80=true other=false badzone=false"
        },
        {
          "claim": "The merged build even accepts a host with a malformed double zone separator that zod 3 rejected.",
          "probe": "const r=o=>o.ok?'ACCEPT':'REJECT';\nconst cfg=h=>({hosts:h,timeoutMs:1000,port:'8080'});\nprint('H3 '+r(oracle.parseConfig(cfg(['fe80::1%eth0%eth1'])))+' '+r(main.parseConfig(cfg(['fe80::1%eth0%eth1']))));",
          "expect": "H3 REJECT ACCEPT"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "heading": "[463] config: timeoutMs was ACCEPTED, now REJECTED",
      "answers": {
        "verdict_changes": true,
        "direction": "stricter",
        "stored_value_changes": false,
        "already_signed_off": false,
        "real_clients": "unknown",
        "why": "timeoutMs = Infinity was accepted by the pre-upgrade code and is rejected by the merged build (probe T1), a one-directional tightening; every example in the group is Infinity. Root cause is in zod itself, not in src/schemas.ts: zod 3's number().positive() treated Infinity as a valid positive number, zod 4's z.number() rejects non-finite values before the positive() check runs (probe POS: z3=true, z4=false; the merged message is 'expected number, received Infinity'). review/corpus-diffs.md never mentions timeoutMs or non-finite numbers: its only ACCEPT NEW is section [2] about age/unsafe integers, and it asserts everything else is 'a pure API rename with no behavioural change' - so this behaviour change is outside the sign-off and the doc's claim does not cover it. Judgement: real_clients = unknown. Infinity cannot travel over JSON, so no HTTP caller can send it, but Config is built in-process from env (port/timeoutMs) and Infinity is a common in-code sentinel for 'no timeout', and Number('Infinity') yields it; the corpus is signups only, so there is no evidence either way. A human should decide whether to keep zod 4's rejection (and give timeoutMs a real .max()) or restore the old acceptance."
      },
      "claims": [
        {
          "claim": "config timeoutMs = Infinity is accepted by the pre-upgrade code and rejected by the merged build.",
          "probe": "const r=o=>o.ok?'ACCEPT':'REJECT';\nconst c={hosts:['1.2.3.4'],timeoutMs:Infinity,port:'8080'};\nprint('T1 '+r(oracle.parseConfig(c))+' '+r(main.parseConfig(c)));\nconst ok={hosts:['1.2.3.4'],timeoutMs:5,port:'8080'};\nprint('T2 '+r(oracle.parseConfig(ok))+' '+r(main.parseConfig(ok)));",
          "expect": "T1 ACCEPT REJECT"
        },
        {
          "claim": "The cause is the library: zod 3 number().positive() accepts Infinity, zod 4 does not.",
          "probe": "const Z3=zod3.z||zod3; const Z4=zod.z||zod;\nprint('POS z3='+Z3.number().positive().safeParse(Infinity).success+' z4='+Z4.number().positive().safeParse(Infinity).success);",
          "expect": "POS z3=true z4=false"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "heading": "[357] signup: ip was REJECTED, now ACCEPTED",
      "answers": {
        "verdict_changes": true,
        "direction": "looser",
        "stored_value_changes": false,
        "already_signed_off": false,
        "real_clients": "yes",
        "why": "Same root cause as the hosts group, on signup.ip: addresses the pre-upgrade code rejected are now accepted (probe I1 '::%1', I2 '2001:db8::ff00:42:8329%25'), and nothing moves the other way. The signed-off change in review/corpus-diffs.md section [4] is specifically about restoring 'fe80::1%eth0' (4 of 400 requests, all the same address), and that case is preserved (probe I3: both accept). But the review's stated justification - link-local addresses are meaningless without a zone - and its factual claim that the union 'reproduces exactly the set zod 3 matched' do not cover what shipped: zonedIpv6 in src/schemas.ts puts a zone on any ipv6 address with any non-empty suffix, and zod 3 only allowed it on fe80 with an alphanumeric zone (probe Z3 in the hosts group). Every example the fuzzer found here is a non-fe80 zoned address or a garbage zone, i.e. exactly the 'nothing new' the human was told would not happen - so already_signed_off = false. Judgement: real_clients = yes for scoped addresses in general (some OS resolvers emit zone IDs on non-link-local addresses), but a well-behaved client would not send 'fe80::1%!!'; the action for a human is to tighten zonedIpv6 to zod 3's shape rather than to re-litigate the ip decision."
      },
      "claims": [
        {
          "claim": "signup.ip values rejected before the upgrade are now accepted, while the signed-off fe80::1%eth0 case still parses in both.",
          "probe": "const B={id:'123e4567-e89b-12d3-a456-426614174000',email:'a@b.co',name:'n',ip:'1.2.3.4',plan:'free',flags:{}};\nconst r=o=>o.ok?'ACCEPT':'REJECT';\nconst t=(tag,v)=>print(tag+' '+r(oracle.parseSignup({...B,ip:v}))+' '+r(main.parseSignup({...B,ip:v})));\nt('I1','::%1'); t('I2','2001:db8::ff00:42:8329%25'); t('I3','fe80::1%eth0');",
          "expect": "I1 REJECT ACCEPT"
        },
        {
          "claim": "The fe80 case the human signed off is unchanged (accepted by both), so the loosening is extra, not the signed-off restoration.",
          "probe": "const B={id:'123e4567-e89b-12d3-a456-426614174000',email:'a@b.co',name:'n',ip:'1.2.3.4',plan:'free',flags:{}};\nconst r=o=>o.ok?'ACCEPT':'REJECT';\nprint('I3 '+r(oracle.parseSignup({...B,ip:'fe80::1%eth0'}))+' '+r(main.parseSignup({...B,ip:'fe80::1%eth0'})));\nprint('I4 '+r(oracle.parseSignup({...B,ip:'fe80::1%!!'}))+' '+r(main.parseSignup({...B,ip:'fe80::1%!!'})));",
          "expect": "I3 ACCEPT ACCEPT"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "heading": "[324] signup: age was ACCEPTED, now REJECTED",
      "answers": {
        "verdict_changes": true,
        "direction": "stricter",
        "stored_value_changes": false,
        "already_signed_off": true,
        "real_clients": "no",
        "why": "Integral floats above Number.MAX_SAFE_INTEGER were accepted before and are rejected now (probe A992 and A1e21), one-directional; the boundary is exactly MAX_SAFE_INTEGER (probe A991: accepted by both), and the merged message is 'Too big: expected int to be <=9007199254740991'. This is precisely review/corpus-diffs.md section [2]: zod 3's .int() was Number.isInteger, zod 4's .int() means safe integer; recommendation ACCEPT NEW with no code change, listing #38 age 1e+21 and #204 age 9007199254740992 - the same values the fuzzer reports. So already_signed_off = true (caveat: the sign-off checkboxes in that file are rendered unticked, but the task states the review was signed off). Judgement: real_clients = no - none of these values is an age, above 2^53 the float grid is coarser than 1 so the stored number is already lossy. The doc's own follow-up (give age a real .max(130)) stays a nice-to-have, not a ship blocker."
      },
      "claims": [
        {
          "claim": "age = 9007199254740992 and 1e+21 were accepted before the upgrade and are rejected now; MAX_SAFE_INTEGER itself is still accepted by both, matching the signed-off section [2].",
          "probe": "const B={id:'123e4567-e89b-12d3-a456-426614174000',email:'a@b.co',name:'n',ip:'1.2.3.4',plan:'free',flags:{}};\nconst r=o=>o.ok?'ACCEPT':'REJECT';\nconst t=(tag,v)=>print(tag+' '+r(oracle.parseSignup({...B,age:v}))+' '+r(main.parseSignup({...B,age:v})));\nt('A991',9007199254740991); t('A992',9007199254740992); t('A1e21',1e21); t('A30',30);",
          "expect": "A992 ACCEPT REJECT"
        },
        {
          "claim": "The cutoff is the safe-integer boundary, not a new domain rule: 9007199254740991 still passes in both builds.",
          "probe": "const B={id:'123e4567-e89b-12d3-a456-426614174000',email:'a@b.co',name:'n',ip:'1.2.3.4',plan:'free',flags:{}};\nconst r=o=>o.ok?'ACCEPT':'REJECT';\nprint('A991 '+r(oracle.parseSignup({...B,age:9007199254740991}))+' '+r(main.parseSignup({...B,age:9007199254740991})));",
          "expect": "A991 ACCEPT ACCEPT"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "heading": "[88] signup: rejected by both, but different fields reported",
      "answers": {
        "verdict_changes": false,
        "direction": "none",
        "stored_value_changes": false,
        "already_signed_off": false,
        "real_clients": "no",
        "why": "Both versions reject every input in this group, so no accept/reject verdict flips and nothing is stored - the only difference is which field names appear in the error list (probe G5a: ip disappears; G5b: age appears). Both movements are shadows of changes already assessed elsewhere: 'ip' drops out because the over-broad zonedIpv6 now accepts the address (group [357]) and 'age' appears because of the signed-off safe-integer tightening (group [324]). The review states the corpus intentionally compares verdict + parsed value + failing field and not message wording (corpus/canon.js), and it does not sign off any change in reported field sets, so already_signed_off = false on this group as such; its two ingredients are covered by groups [357] (not signed off) and [324] (signed off). Judgement: real_clients = no - every example fails between 2 and 8 fields at once, which is generated garbage rather than a well-behaved client, and the client-visible consequence (a 400 naming slightly different fields) needs no separate action once the ip over-breadth in group [357] is fixed, which will also restore most of these field lists."
      },
      "claims": [
        {
          "claim": "An input rejected by both builds reports different field sets, and 'ip' vanishing is caused by the newly accepted zoned address.",
          "probe": "const B={id:'123e4567-e89b-12d3-a456-426614174000',email:'a@b.co',name:'n',ip:'1.2.3.4',plan:'free',flags:{}};\nconst f=o=>o.ok?'OK':o.problems.map(p=>String(p).split(':')[0]).sort().join(',');\nconst i1={...B,email:'nope',ip:'2001:db8::1%eth0'};\nprint('G5a o='+f(oracle.parseSignup(i1))+' m='+f(main.parseSignup(i1)));",
          "expect": "G5a o=email,ip m=email"
        },
